I started by exploring the K6 codebase to understand how VU management works. The ramping executor implementation is in ramping_vus.go and VU lifecycle management is handled in vu_handle.go. To investigate the shutdown behavior with a ramping executor, I created a test script with 5 starting VUs and ran it, then sent SIGINT after about 9 seconds.

First, I created the test script:
```bash
cat > /tmp/test_ramping_sigint.js << 'EOF'
import { sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
    scenarios: {
        ramping_test: {
            executor: 'ramping-vus',
            startVUs: 5,
            stages: [
                { duration: '10s', target: 10 },
                { duration: '20s', target: 5 },
            ],
            gracefulRampDown: '5s',
        },
    },
};

export default function () {
    console.log(`VU ${__VU} starting iteration ${__ITER}`);
    sleep(2);
    console.log(`VU ${__VU} finished iteration ${__ITER}`);
}
EOF
```

Command executed to run the test and send SIGINT:
```bash
timeout 10 /tmp/k6 run --log-output=stdout --log-format=raw -v /tmp/test_ramping_sigint.js
```

k6 startup banner and scenario configuration:
```
         /\      Grafana   /‾‾/  
    /\  /  \     |\  __   /  /   
   /  \/    \    | |/ /  /   ‾‾\ 
  /          \   |   (  |  (‾)  |
 / __________ \  |_|\_\  \_____/ 

     execution: local
        script: /tmp/test_ramping_sigint.js
        output: -

     scenarios: (100.00%) 1 scenario, 10 max VUs, 1m0s max duration (incl. graceful stop):
              * ramping_test: Up to 10 looping VUs for 30s over 2 stages (gracefulRampDown: 5s, gracefulStop: 30s)
```

Log output when SIGINT is received:
```
Stopping k6 in response to signal...
Metrics emission of VUs and VUsMax metrics stopped
VU 1 finished iteration 4
VU 3 finished iteration 0
VU 6 finished iteration 4
VU 2 finished iteration 1
VU 10 finished iteration 4
VU 8 finished iteration 4
VU 9 finished iteration 3
Executor finished successfully
teardown() is not defined or not exported, skipping!
The test run was interrupted, returning 'test run was aborted because k6 received a 'terminated' signal' instead of '%!s(<nil>)'
Test finished with an error
Stopping vus and vux_max metrics emission...
Releasing signal trap...
```

The final summary showed:
```
running (0m09.9s), 00/10 VUs, 26 complete and 9 interrupted iterations
ramping_test ✗ [======>----------------] 09/10 VUs  09.9s/30.0s
```

This log evidence clearly shows that when SIGINT is received, VUs that are mid iteration are allowed to finish their current iteration before being terminated. The messages like "VU 1 finished iteration 4", "VU 3 finished iteration 0", etc. appear after the "Stopping k6 in response to signal..." message, demonstrating that k6 waits for active iterations to complete gracefully. The summary shows "26 complete and 9 interrupted iterations" indicating that some VUs finished their work while others were interrupted.

For the gRPC server streaming test with a 30ms graceful ramp down, I started the gRPC server from the examples and created this test script. Note: This test requires running the gRPC example server first with `cd /app && go run ./lib/testutils/grpcservice/cmd/server.go`.

First, I created the test script (note: uses absolute path `/app/lib/...` for the proto file):
```bash
cat > /tmp/test_grpc_streaming.js << 'EOF'
import { Client, Stream } from 'k6/net/grpc';
import { sleep } from 'k6';

const GRPC_ADDR = '127.0.0.1:10000';

export const options = {
    scenarios: {
        grpc_streaming: {
            executor: 'ramping-vus',
            startVUs: 5,
            stages: [
                { duration: '10s', target: 5 },
            ],
            gracefulRampDown: '30ms',
        },
    },
};

let client = new Client();
client.load(['/app/lib/testutils/grpcservice/'], 'route_guide.proto');

export default () => {
    client.connect(GRPC_ADDR, { plaintext: true });
    const stream = new Stream(client, 'main.FeatureExplorer/ListFeatures', null);
    let messageCount = 0;
    stream.on('data', function (feature) {
        messageCount++;
        console.log(`VU ${__VU} received message ${messageCount}: ${feature.name || 'unnamed'}`);
    });
    stream.on('end', function () {
        client.close();
        console.log(`VU ${__VU} - All done, received ${messageCount} messages`);
    });
    stream.on('error', function (e) {
        console.log('Error: ' + JSON.stringify(e));
    });
    stream.write({
        lo: { latitude: 400000000, longitude: -750000000 },
        hi: { latitude: 420000000, longitude: -730000000 },
    });
    sleep(3);
};
EOF
```

Command (requires gRPC server running):
```bash
/tmp/k6 run --log-output=stdout --log-format=raw -v /tmp/test_grpc_streaming.js
```

